        <Options>
            <Main applyHouseholdOperatingConditions="false" applyReducedOperatingConditions="false" atypicalElectricalLoads="false" waterConservation="false" referenceHouse="false">
                <Vermiculite code="1">
                    <English>Unknown</English>
                    <French>Inconnu</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

Overall, your house is reasonably air tight, however there are some key areas where air leakage occurs. Air leakage was observed through the weather stripping along the three doors, underneath the garage door threshold, and around the attic hatch. The weather stripping around the doors should be replaced.

The attic hatch and door threshold could be sealed using caulking, 'Draft Attack'  is a removable caulking which could be used to seal the attic hatch and door threshold. 



Air leakage was also observed through the rangehood fan. When you are considering replacing the rangehood, I recommend you replace your rangehood with an ENERGY STAR® rated model.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 160&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;5.6 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a home energy evaluation, which includes an air leakage test, before and after your retrofits. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 studs with 6 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation, as well as 2x2 strapping with no insulation. I have modelled adding 3 inches (R15) of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding R15 of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 550&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;18.6 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,875 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>

The attic of your house was assumed to have 9 inches (R31) blown cellullose insulation. I have modelled adding an additional 5 inches of blown cellullose to achieve R50.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding 5 inches blown cellullose insulation to the attic, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 120&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;4.1 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 125 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total ceiling area and achieve a minimum insulation value of RSI 7.0 (R-40). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of ceiling area that is insulated compared to the total ceiling area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</ceilings>
                <foundation>

Your basement pony walls appear to be 2x6 studs with 6 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation, as well as 2x2 strapping with no insulation. In conjuction with the main house exterior walls,I have modelled adding 3 inches (R15) of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation to the pony walls. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding 3 inches (R15) of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation to the pony walls, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 200&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;6.9 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 343.75 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total foundation wall area including crawlspaces (when applicable), and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.8 (R-10). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</foundation>
                <windows>

As you are considering replacing the windows and patio door in the living room/dining room, as well as the small window in the laundry room, I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading all of them with ENERGY STAR® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 



Please keep in mind, the window upgrade savings below do not include air sealing savings in the replacement. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing the three windows, and patio door with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STAR® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 180&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;6.2 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</windows>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 182 litre electric hot water tank, which is also indirectly heated by the boiler. Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly.

It was mentioned that you shut the boiler down during the summer months, and run the electric hot water tank. 

As you currently have a very efficient oil boiler, it would be ideal to use your boiler to heat your hot water year round. This can be done by lowering the thermostats around the home, and leaving the boiler operational during the summer as well. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By using your boiler to heat your hot water year round, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 230&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs. 

&lt;b&gt;Please note&lt;/b&gt;: Winter is assumed to be 9 months of the year.

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Boiler&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



Your current oil fired boiler was originally rated at 86% AFUE. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR® qualified high-efficiency oil boiler with a minimum efficiency of 87% AFUE. If you choose a high-efficiency oil furnace (direct vented out a side wall), the current chimney can be eliminated altogether.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a sealed combustion, oil-fired boiler with an efficiency of 87% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 350&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;11.8 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Combustion Air Supply&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



There was a 5 inch combustion air supply pipe to provide combustion air to the boiler and hot water tank observed during the evaluation. I recommend you have an electric damper installed to ensure combustion air will only be introduced into the room when the boiler is operating.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing an electric damper for the combustion air supply, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 190&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;6.4 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. 



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Pellet Stove&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



If you decide to install a pellet stove, I recommend you install an EPA Certified pellet stove. Ensure that the installation complies with local building and fire codes and includes a dedicated combustion air duct. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By installing an EPA Certified pellet stove and using it for 50% of your heating load, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 560 &lt;/b&gt; per year in energy savings, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; (or 1/3 of pre-tax purchase cost of the stove), from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Electronically Communicated Motors&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency.  These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to learn the systems usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house you can qualify for &lt;b&gt;$ 175 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;





&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Yellowknife&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.45 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil  - $1.12 per litre 

Propane - $0.71 per litre

Wood - $341 per cord 

Pellets - $5.67 per bag



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

